# Boy Scout rule

Leave the repository in a better state than you found it in every session.

- Improve the code you touch when a focused cleanup makes it easier to maintain.
- Prefer small modules with one clear responsibility and shared implementations
  over copied logic, repeated setup, or parallel code paths for the same behavior.
- Avoid duplicated API calls, database queries, subprocesses, validation and
  initialization. Reuse existing results within their valid lifetime; coalesce
  concurrent identical work when authority, ownership and cancellation permit it.
- Keep checks at meaningful approval, I/O, mutation and delivery boundaries.
  Do not remove a necessary authorization/revocation check as an optimization,
  or cache authority across a boundary that can make it stale.
- Consolidate repeated test fixtures while retaining real consumer-path coverage.
- Keep cleanup proportional to the task. Preserve public behavior and required
  tests, make changes reviewable, and explain any deliberate tradeoff.
